# Exposure

Stop when this session **opens** a forbidden page or file, or **pastes or keeps** forbidden body from [clean-room.mdc](clean-room.mdc) or [clean-room-third-party-sources.mdc](clean-room-third-party-sources.mdc): leaked source, the Windows Research Kernel, disassembly, decompiler output, symbol data, or WDK or SDK header text.

A search hit that only shows a title, URL, or adjacent marketing blurb is not exposure. Do not open that result. Do not quote or keep any forbidden body that a tool inlined next to it. Prefer `site:learn.microsoft.com` paths under `/windows/` and `/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/`. Skip code-hosting sites that are not Wine, ReactOS, or this fork. Skip Learn pages under `/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/` and other debugger, symbol, or dump pages.

Opening the forbidden page, pasting its body, or keeping any of that body in a scratch file or the patch is exposure. Then stop. Do not read further. Do not summarize it, quote it, or continue the component it touches in this session.

Tell Ryan in chat with:

- the date
- the URL or file path where it appeared, with no content from it
- the component and the files the session was working on
- whether any patch was already written after it appeared

Append the same lines to `contamination-log.md` at the project root, outside `source/`. Do not commit that file.

A component named in the log is written again in a new session that has not seen the material and is not given the log entry's details beyond the component name. Discard any uncommitted hunk written after the exposure. Ryan decides what happens to a committed one. Ryan may clear a quarantine in the log when the hit was only an unopened search result under this rule.

```text
Right: ignore a SERP title for a debugger page; open the DDI Learn page instead
Right: stop after opening a forbidden page or pasting its body, report the URL, log it
Wrong: open the debuggercmds hit "just to check"
Wrong: quote symbol names from a search snippet in chat or in the patch
```
